       wwiinniiccoonnttooppaamm  [--iimmaaggee==_i_n_d_e_x  |   --aalllliimmaaggeess]   [--aannddmmaasskkss]   [--vveerrbboossee]
       [_i_c_o_n___f_i_l_e]

       All  options  can  be abbreviated to their shortest unique prefix.  You
       may use two hyphens instead of one to designate an option.  You may use
       either  white  space  or  equals  signs  between an option name and its
       value.


DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       wwiinniiccoonnttooppaamm reads a Microsoft Windows icon file and converts it to one
       or more RGB_ALPHA Netpbm PAM images.

       There  are  two  kinds of transparency information a a Windows icon can
       have: and mask and graded transparency map.  The and mask is older  and
       indicates  for each pixel whether it is fully opaque or fully transpar‐
       ent.  The graded transparency map can indicate translucent values  too.
       The  graded transparency map is present in an icon that uses PNG encod‐
       ing or uses BMP encoding with 32 bits per pixel.

       The transparency plane in the PAM output of wwiinniiccoonnttooppaamm comes from
         the graded transparency map if it exists and the and mask otherwise.

       An icon may actually have both forms of transparency information.   See
       --aannddmmaasskkss.

       The output goes to Standard Output.

       The  output is a multi-image PAM file.  If you want a separate file for
       each image, use ppaammsspplliitt(1).



OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common  Options  ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), wwiinniiccoonnttooppaamm recognizes
       the following command line options:




       --aalllliimmaaggeess
              Convert all images in the input.

              You may not specify this together with --iimmaaggee.

              By default, wwiinniiccoonnttooppaamm converts only the  one  "best  quality"
              image (largest, then most bits per pixel).


       --iimmaaggee==_i_n_d_e_x
              Convert image number _i_n_d_e_x (starting with zero).

              You may not specify this together with --aalllliimmaaggeess.

              By  default,  wwiinniiccoonnttooppaamm  converts  the  "best  quality" image
              (largest, then most bits per pixel).


       --aannddmmaasskkss
              If the image to be extracted contains both  graded  transparency
              data  and  an  AND  mask, produce five-channel Netpbm PAM images
              with the AND mask as the fifth plane.  An  image  that  formally
              has  graded  transparency  data,  but  it  indicates nothing but
              opaque pixels is considered  not  to  have  graded  transparency
              data, so --aannddmmaasskkss would have no effect on it.


       --vveerrbboossee
              Print more messages





